 ### [Analytic 3D Gaussian Ray Tracing for Tomography](3017-analytic-3d-gaussian-ray-tracing-for-tomography.md)
-This method replaces local affine approximations with exact ray tracing to compute line integrals through 3D Gaussian primitives analytically. It addresses quantitative accuracy degradation and geometric inflexibility in tomographic reconstruction, such as nonlinear arc-corrections in PET scanners. Unlike splatting-based pipelines that project into 2D screen space, this approach directly computes ray-primitive intersections, avoiding the assumption of non-overlapping Gaussians.
+This method replaces the local affine approximation of splatting with exact ray tracing to compute line integrals through 3D Gaussian primitives analytically. It solves the problem of quantitative accuracy degradation and geometric inflexibility in tomographic reconstruction, specifically addressing nonlinear corrections like arc-correction in PET scanners. Unlike splatting-based pipelines which project into 2D screen space, this approach directly computes ray-primitive intersections, avoiding the assumption of non-overlapping Gaussians.
 
 ### CT Initialization via Fan Beam Ray Transform and FBP
-This variant initializes CT reconstruction by computing a reference image via Fan beam ray transform and filtered back projection (FBP), then creates a sparse sinogram by subsampling and resizing [S3, unofficial]. Steps include: 1. Converting input to CPU numpy array. 2. Initializing sinogram, sparse_sinogram, and ori_img arrays with shape [batch, 720, 720] [S3, unofficial]. 3. Computing sinogram via Fan_ray_trafo. 4. Computing reference image ori_img via Fan_FBP [S3, unofficial]. 5. Storing sinogram max value. 6. Subsampling sinogram along the first axis with stride $r$ [S3, unofficial]. 7. Resizing to [720, 720] using cv2.resize [S3, unofficial]. Parameter $r$ is the subsampling stride. Output arrays have shape [batch, 720, 720] and dtype float32 [S3, unofficial].
